\paragraph{Relative severity shifts at 355M.} At 355M, model collapse in the Uncurated baseline is substantially more severe (PPL~91.66) than at 125M ($47.04 \pm 2.90$, $n{=}3$). Both 50\%-budget methods achieve lower Generation~7 perplexity than Uncurated: Random~50\% reaches 70.25 ($-$23\%) and RGDS reaches 82.84 ($-$10\%). This contrasts with the 125M setting, where RGDS \emph{increased} perplexity by 58\% relative to Uncurated ($74.29$ vs.\ $47.04$, $n{=}3$). However, 50\%-budget methods train on half the data volume; the perplexity advantage over Uncurated therefore conflates any selection benefit with a data quantity effect, and we cannot attribute the improvement to curation quality alone.

\paragraph{Alignment gap narrows but persists.} The RGDS selection ratio at Generation~7 is 0.83 at 355M, compared to $0.64 \pm 0.20$ at 125M ($n{=}3$). The ratio remains below~1.0---RGDS still preferentially retains content-poor samples---but the inversion is less severe. The +18\% perplexity penalty relative to Random (vs.\ +72\% at 125M) is consistent with this narrower gap.%
